## Reading config — always through the resolver
Config lives in THREE tiers, and only the first is committed team config:

| Tier | File | Holds |
|---|---|---|
| 1 team | `.claude/config/stack.yaml` | which tool fills each seam, which data the team reads, policies, ticket conventions |
| 2 person, portable | `people/<id>.yaml` (committed) | display name, identities, comms voice, file-type preferences |
| 3 person, machine | `.claude/config/connections.local.yaml` (gitignored) | named profiles/connections, local mount roots |

**Never read `stack.yaml` directly.** Raw file, raw answer: you would see the team's values and miss
every personal and machine override, which is exactly the case the tiers exist for. Read the merged
result instead — and note the resolver may report a value the raw file does not contain at all, such
as a docstore `base_path` composed from a team folder and your own mount root:

```bash
bash "${CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT:-$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || echo .)}/bin/tw" effective_config.py --root . --json
```

It needs no agent-specific environment variable, so this works under any harness. `provenance` names
which tier supplied each key. Exit codes: 0 ok · 3 missing · 4 malformed · 5 stale · 6 prohibited
override.

Tier 3 selects CREDENTIALS AND LOCAL PATHS ONLY. It can never change which catalog, schema,
database or target is read — two teammates must never silently read different data — and it can
never contribute a `policies:` block, so a gitignored file no one reviews cannot switch a safety
gate off. Both rules are enforced in code, not documented: a violation is rejected, not ignored.

## Permission hierarchy
**No permission needed (internal):** read queries / data exploration; reading/searching/writing files
in the repo; local analysis + output.
**Explicit permission required (external/destructive):** high-risk DB writes; commits/pushes/PRs;
tracker comments; chat messages; docstore backups.

**DB-write protocol:** show the exact SQL → explain the change → wait for `yes` → run.
(No warehouse seam in this repo? This whole section is inert — there are no DB writes to gate.)

How much is gated is set by `policies.db_write_requires_approval` in `.claude/config/stack.yaml`:

- `off` — nothing is gated.
- `high_risk` *(default)* — gate anything irreversible, access-changing, or unrecognized:
  `DROP`, `TRUNCATE`, `DELETE`, `UPDATE`, `MERGE`, `GRANT`/`REVOKE`, every `CREATE OR REPLACE`,
  and every `ALTER` other than `ALTER … ADD`. Plain `CREATE`, `INSERT INTO`, `ALTER … ADD`, and
  `COMMENT ON` are additive — run them without asking.
- `all` — gate every mutation, additive ones included.

Read the current value before assuming; don't hardcode one of these behaviors.

In Claude Code this is enforced mechanically by the `db_write_guard` PreToolUse hook
(`.claude/hooks/`), which sees SQL hidden in a `-f` file or a stdin redirect too, so the rule holds
even if the agent forgets it. **For every other agent it is guidance, not enforcement** — hooks are
the only mechanically enforced layer, so honoring this protocol is on you.

## The 10 policies (inherited by every skill, from stack.yaml)
1. **hard_halt_before_external_posts** — pause for human go before any tracker/chat/docstore write.
2. **db_write_requires_approval** — `off` | `high_risk` (default) | `all`; show SQL, explain, wait.
3. **chat_default_draft** — draft, don't send, unless told "send it".
4. **hyperlink_everything** — every ticket id / file / PR is a smart link.
5. **skillify_everything** — recurring work becomes a `/productize` skill the agent can invoke itself.
6. **reduce_assumptions** — ask before building; still document every assumption in the ticket README.
7. **commit_plan_before_implement** — `/spec-and-build` commits the spec before building (blame-free retry).
8. **system_evolution** — a failure fixes the AI layer (rule/context/command/adapter), not just the ticket.
9. **deterministic_outputs** — explicit `ORDER BY` on exports; productized skills ship golden-replay diffs.
10. **human_review_handoff** — `off` | `review` (default) | `all`; open the deliverables in the
    human's own apps and **wait for sign-off** before the verdict. Which apps is per-user and
    gitignored (`.claude/config/viewer.local.yaml`); run `bash bin/handoff.sh <paths>`. Every other
    pause guards something leaving the machine — this one makes sure a person actually saw the
    output. If no viewer config exists, say so once and carry on; never block on it.

## Ticket folder
```
{{ticket_path}}/
├── README.md            # business context + ALL assumptions + QC results
├── source_materials/    # attachments / inputs
├── final_deliverables/  # numbered in review order; filenames carry record counts
├── qc_queries/          # numbered QC + the /review verdict
└── exploratory_analysis/
```

## Deliverable exports (CSV)
*Applies to tabular exports; document/report deliverables follow the same spirit — deterministic,
review-ready files.*
**ASCII punctuation only in cell values** — no em/en dashes, smart quotes, or ellipsis characters;
they render as mojibake in Excel and many CSV viewers (use a plain `-`, or "to" for ranges). Header
row first (no preamble/blank rows), filename carries the record count, explicit `ORDER BY` so re-runs
are byte-identical.

## Communication (limits)
Tracker comment <{{wl_tracker_comment}} words · chat <{{wl_chat}} · PR <{{wl_pr}} · new ticket
<{{wl_ticket}}>. Tracker comments: business-first, segmented with counts/%/$, link the specific
docstore file. Chat: include {{chat_always_include}}; hyperlink everything; default to draft.
When a **voice profile** is configured for the shipper (a `voice:` block in `people/<id>.yaml`), `/ship` phrases
comms drafts to match it — but only *within* the limits above; the profile never overrides a word
cap, a hyperlink, or the include-list. No profile ⇒ drafting is unchanged. Build one with
`/setup --voice`.

## Quality control (the validation pyramid)
Every deliverable: ① dialect lint → ② counts & dedup (duplicate detection is the primary test) →
③ cross-source reconciliation → ④ independent re-run + anti-pattern sweep → ⑤ human sign-off.
No warehouse seam (or non-query deliverables): skip the layers with nothing to run; `/review` runs
layer ⑤ plus a claims-vs-evidence walk instead. Run `/review` before `/ship`.

## Git
Branch = ticket id off `{{default_branch}}`. Semantic commits/PRs (`feat|fix|docs|refactor|chore|
test|ci`). Stage only files you changed. Co-Authored-By trailer on commits.

**Repo-setup / AI-layer files get their own commit — never folded into a ticket's feature commit.**
These belong to the *repo's* plugin setup, not to any one ticket: `.claude/settings.json`,
`.claude/config/stack.yaml`, `.claude/statusline.sh`, `AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md`,
`documentation/AI_LAYER_INDEX.md`, and `.gitignore`. When `/setup` or a plugin `autoUpdate` leaves
any of them dirty while a ticket is in flight, give them a separate `chore(plugins): …` commit **on
the same branch as the ticket** — a distinct commit (clean history, easy revert) that rides the
ticket's **one** PR, so there's never a second PR to merge. (A standalone setup change with no ticket
open is its own small PR to `{{default_branch}}`.) The enablement travels with the repo, so teammates
who trust it are prompted to install the plugin. Personal / machine-local config stays gitignored and
is never committed: `.claude/settings.local.json`, `.claude/worktrees/`.
